
Understood on the split, and I'll work architecture-side only from here. One collision to resolve first, because the boundary as drawn cuts through a container I just handed you.

## The stamp mixes both layers

`<stamp>` specifies fire conditions, a hard cap, and exemptions — architecture. It also specifies the literal strings `INTERPRETATION` and `Proceeding.` — UX. As written, UX cannot rename the heading to "Current Interpretation" without editing an architecture container, and architecture cannot tighten the cap without touching text UX owns. Same problem in the readback headings, the scale log line, and the trailer labels.

Fix is an indirection layer. Architecture names slots by ID; UX owns the rendered string per ID.

````
<projection>
Architecture defines slots. UX defines strings. One table, two owners.

S1   stamp heading            "INTERPRETATION"
S2   stamp terminator         "Proceeding."
S3   stamp overflow           "Reading exceeds stamp capacity — /slow recommended."
R1-n readback headings        (existing labels)
T1   trailer: assumptions
T2   trailer: bounds tripped
T3   trailer: questions
T4   trailer: proposals
L1   scale log                "24 slides requested; 24 spined, 7 built."
C1   continuation header      (stub headline, verbatim from spine)

UX may change any string freely. UX may not change slot count, slot order, fire conditions,
caps, or budgets.

One constraint on strings: a string may not add content beyond its slot's declared payload.
Renaming S1 is UX. A heading that asserts something about the artifact is an architecture change
and comes back here.
</projection>
````

This makes your "Current Interpretation" preference a one-cell edit later, and it means the projection review you've deferred can happen without reopening anything settled.

## Which checks apply to which text

Related gap, and it needs closing before calibration or UX can proceed independently. The firewall now governs the artifact's own sentences at production severity. The stamp, readback, and trailer are also sentences. If firewall entries apply to them, UX rewording can trip enforcement; if calibration tightens a hedge budget, it constrains question phrasing UX owns.

````
<apparatus>
Apparatus = stamp, readback, trailer, scale log, continuation headers, spine listings.
Apparatus is not artifact.

Firewall, mechanic tables (U/D/P), adapter constraints, register spec: artifact only.
Apparatus is governed by its own caps plus A1–A3:

A1  No apparatus line asserts anything the artifact does not support.
A2  No apparatus line describes the artifact's structure beyond its slot's declared payload.
    (U12 governs artifact-side; A2 is its apparatus analogue.)
A3  Apparatus never narrates the spec layer in artifact mode.

Consequence: UX rewording cannot trip a firewall entry, and firewall calibration cannot
constrain UX phrasing. The layers are now independently editable.
</apparatus>
````

## 1. Firewall calibration

The blocker is that thresholds can't be validated until measurement is reproducible, and right now several aren't. "40% three-bullet slides" needs a pinned definition of *content slide* — does a section divider count in the denominator? A transition slide with one line? Until the denominator is fixed, two people counting the same deck get different numbers, and the calibration data is worthless.

So the general form first, which also subsumes the D3/D4 low-N patch:

````
<rates>
Every entry expressed as a rate or budget declares four fields:
  numerator definition · denominator definition · minimum denominator · status

Status is #asserted or #calibrated.
  #asserted  — trips log to the trailer, do not enter two-pass repair
  #calibrated — trips bind, enter two-pass repair

Below minimum denominator an entry does not apply. It does not scale down.
Absolute entries declare no denominator and apply at any length.

Promotion to #calibrated requires the entry's natural rate measured across ≥3 real artifacts
that pass qualitative review independent of the check.
</rates>
````

Everything currently numeric ships `#asserted`. That is the part I'd push hardest: it lets assembly complete now without pretending the numbers are validated, and every trip logged in the trailer is a calibration datapoint. Shipping generates the corpus.

Applying it to the four entries whose exposure I flagged:

- **D3** — numerator: slides whose body is exactly three bullets. Denominator: content slides, excluding dividers and single-line transitions. Minimum denominator 8. Below 8: fails at 4+ *consecutive*.
- **D4** — same denominator, minimum 8. Below 8: does not apply.
- **U6, U7** — per 500 words of artifact prose, excluding headings, captions, and apparatus. Minimum 250 words.
- **P4, P5** — P5's 1 per 150 is the one I'd most expect to be wrong, and it's the tightest ratio in the set, so it will trip most often and calibrate fastest.

The calibration run itself: build 3–4 real tasks per adapter with checks in log-only mode, count natural rates, and set each threshold above what a qualitatively good artifact produces. The direction matters — if a good artifact trips a threshold, the threshold is wrong. Calibrating to catch bad artifacts while a good one sits just under is the only correct shape, and you can't find that line by reasoning about it.

## 2. Assembly

Load order, with the two new containers placed:

1. identity and purpose
2. tracks, adapters
3. `<delivery>`
4. lifecycle Steps 1–4
5. `<readback>`, `<stamp>`, trailer — `<apparatus>` attaches here
6. firewall
7. mechanic tables (U/D/P) — `<rates>` attaches here
8. `<scale>`
9. `<proposals>`
10. `<projection>` last, since it only resolves IDs defined above

Single dispatch at Step 4, reading three flags — track, delivery mode, speed — and branching once. No branch logic anywhere else.

**The remaining architectural gap,** and I think it's the last one: there is no declared precedence order for conflicts. `<scale>` created a live instance. If 7 of 24 slides are built, does D4's denominator count built slides or spined ones? And on continuation, does a cumulative measure trip against text already shipped, which cannot be repaired?

Proposed resolution:

> Rate entries measure built artifact surface only. Spined stubs are not surface.
> On continuation, measure cumulatively across all built segments. Repair only within the current segment. If a trip is caused entirely by standing text from an earlier segment, log it — do not rebuild shipped text.
> Precedence, highest first: user intent → firewall → adapter hard constraint → `<scale>` ceiling → mechanic budget → proposal.

`<scale>` outranking mechanic budgets is deliberate: better a truncated build that logs a budget trip than a padded one that satisfies it.

## 3. Readback

Closed. Mechanics are settled, R6a/R14/R2 are in, the stamp is specified, continuation inheritance is specified. Remaining work is slot-ID substitution per `<projection>`, which is assembly-mechanical.

## 4. Proposals, architecturally

The failure mode you're describing has a structural cause: if a proposal slot exists and wants filling, the cheapest fill is an implementation alternative, because those are always available. Architecture's job is to make the slot expensive to fill and cheap to leave empty.

````
<proposals>
Default out. Trailer only, after the artifact. Max 2. Zero is the expected case, not a shortfall.

Admission test — all four must hold:
Pa  Source: derives from the gap between spec and exemplar corpus, not from an unresolved
    spec field. If it would have been a QUESTION had you not decided it, it is a question.
    Route it there.
Pb  Severability: the artifact as shipped is complete without it. A proposal is never a hole.
Pc  Non-substitution: it was not silently implemented. Built artifact = stated intent.
Pd  Cost named: length, register shift, scope, or work landing on the user.

Reject on sight:
    adapter capability restatements ("Canva could also…")
    format swaps carrying no change in argument
    anything reachable by the user rereading their own brief

Admit by preference order:
1  an argument turn the material supports and the brief did not ask for
2  evidence or framing the exemplar corpus uses and the brief omits
3  an adjacent artifact the request implies but does not name
4  a register move, axis and step named

One proposal set per artifact, on the segment containing the close. Earlier continuation
segments carry none.
</proposals>
````

Pa is the load-bearing one. It's the test that separates proposals from questions structurally rather than by tone, and it's what keeps the mechanism from degrading into a second questions block. The preference order does the rest of the work: class 1 requires you to have understood the material well enough to see a turn in it, which is exactly the capability you want the mechanism to express, and it is not reachable by listing what the adapter can do.

**Cost:** `<projection>` ~110 tokens, `<apparatus>` ~90, `<rates>` ~120, `<proposals>` ~180. `<rates>` pays for itself by letting D3/D4/U6/U7/P4/P5 ship provisional rather than blocking assembly on a calibration run. `<projection>` is net-neutral now and saves a full container rewrite when the UX pass lands.

Next concrete step is the precedence ladder — confirm or amend, since assembly's dispatch depends on it and it's the only item I'd still call architectural.
